### What is the resolution order for each annotation?

Each injection annotation resolves bean ambiguity in a different order:

| Step | @Autowired (Spring) | @Resource (Jakarta) | @Inject (CDI) |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1st | Type match | name attribute | Type match |
| 2nd | @Qualifier | Field/property name | @Named |
| 3rd | Field/parameter name | Type fallback | Field/parameter name |
| 4th | @Primary | Exception | Exception |
| 5th | Exception | | |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import jakarta.annotation.Resource;
import jakarta.inject.Inject;
import jakarta.inject.Named;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;

// @Autowired: TYPE first, then @Qualifier
public class ServiceA {
    @Autowired
    @Qualifier("jpaAccountRepository")
    private AccountRepository repo;
}

// @Resource: NAME first, then type
public class ServiceB {
    @Resource(name = "jpaAccountRepository")
    private AccountRepository repo;
}

// @Inject: TYPE first, then @Named
public class ServiceC {
    @Inject
    @Named("jpaAccountRepository")
    private AccountRepository repo;
}
```

All three examples inject the same bean, but the resolution strategy differs. `@Autowired` starts from the type and narrows by qualifier. `@Resource` starts from the name and falls back to type. `@Inject` behaves like `@Autowired` but uses `@Named` instead of `@Qualifier`.

### Why can @Resource NOT do constructor injection?

The `@Resource` annotation's `@Target` includes `TYPE`, `FIELD`, and `METHOD` but does not include `PARAMETER`. Constructor injection works by annotating constructor parameters, which requires `ElementType.PARAMETER` in the target. Since `@Resource` lacks this, the compiler rejects it on constructor parameters.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import jakarta.annotation.Resource;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class TransferService {
    // WRONG -- does not compile
    // public TransferService(@Resource(name = "jpaRepo") AccountRepository repo) { }
    //                        ^^^^^^^^^ @Resource not applicable to PARAMETER

    // @Resource works on fields and setters only
    @Resource(name = "jpaAccountRepository")
    private AccountRepository repo;
}
```

This is a significant limitation because constructor injection is the recommended style. Use `@Autowired` with `@Qualifier` on constructor parameters when you need name-based resolution:

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class TransferService {
    private final AccountRepository repo;

    // CORRECT -- @Qualifier on constructor parameter
    public TransferService(
            @Qualifier("jpaAccountRepository") AccountRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}
```

### How do you do DI without any annotation?

Since Spring 4.3, a class with a single constructor does not need `@Autowired`. Spring auto-detects it and resolves all parameters from the container. Combined with Lombok, this is the cleanest style:

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import com.bank.repository.AuditRepository;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class TransferService {
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final AuditRepository auditRepo;

    // No @Autowired, no @Inject, no @Resource
    // Lombok generates the constructor, Spring auto-detects it

    @Transactional
    public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        var sender = accountRepo.findById(from).orElseThrow();
        var receiver = accountRepo.findById(to).orElseThrow();
        sender.debit(amount);
        receiver.credit(amount);
        accountRepo.save(sender);
        accountRepo.save(receiver);
        auditRepo.log(from, to, amount);
    }
}
```

```text
Transfer completed: SAV2001 -> SAV2002, amount=10000
Audit logged: SAV2001 -> SAV2002, amount=10000
```

### How is @Autowired different from @Resource and @Inject?

| Feature | @Autowired | @Resource | @Inject |
|---|---|---|---|
| Package | `org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation` | `jakarta.annotation` | `jakarta.inject` |
| Standard | Spring proprietary | Jakarta JSR-250 | Jakarta CDI (JSR-330) |
| Resolution | Type first | Name first | Type first |
| Disambiguation | `@Qualifier` | `name` attribute | `@Named` |
| Optional deps | `required = false` | No | No |
| Constructor | Yes | No (missing PARAMETER target) | Yes |
| Field | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Setter | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Spring coupling | Yes | No | No |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import jakarta.annotation.Resource;
import jakarta.inject.Inject;
import jakarta.inject.Named;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

// All three inject the same bean differently:

@Service
public class AutowiredExample {
    @Autowired
    @Qualifier("jpaAccountRepository")
    private AccountRepository repo;
}

@Service
public class ResourceExample {
    @Resource(name = "jpaAccountRepository")
    private AccountRepository repo;
}

@Service
public class InjectExample {
    @Inject
    @Named("jpaAccountRepository")
    private AccountRepository repo;
}
```

### When should you use each annotation?

- **No annotation (single constructor):** the modern default and cleanest approach. Use this for all new code.
- **@Autowired:** when you need `required = false` for optional dependencies, or when a class has multiple constructors and you need to tell Spring which one to use.
- **@Resource:** for legacy Java EE interoperability where name-based resolution is needed. Avoid for new code because it cannot do constructor injection.
- **@Inject:** for Jakarta CDI compatibility in mixed CDI and Spring codebases. Rarely needed in Spring Boot applications.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private NotificationService notificationService; // optional

    public ReportService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Autowired(required = false) // only @Autowired supports this
    public void setNotificationService(NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    public void generateReport(String accountId) {
        var account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        String report = "Balance: " + account.getBalance();
        if (notificationService != null) {
            notificationService.send(accountId, report);
        }
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with injection annotations?

**WRONG -- using @Resource on a constructor parameter:**

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import jakarta.annotation.Resource;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class TransferService {
    private final AccountRepository repo;

    // WRONG -- does not compile
    // @Resource cannot target PARAMETER
    public TransferService(@Resource AccountRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}
```

```text
error: annotation @Resource is not applicable in this context
```

**CORRECT -- use @Qualifier with constructor injection:**

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class TransferService {
    private final AccountRepository repo;

    public TransferService(
            @Qualifier("jpaAccountRepository") AccountRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}
```

**WRONG -- mixing @Inject and @Qualifier:**

```java
// @Inject uses @Named, not @Qualifier
@Inject
@Qualifier("smsService") // Spring-specific, not CDI standard
private NotificationService notification;
```

**CORRECT -- @Inject with @Named:**

```java
@Inject
@Named("smsNotificationService") // CDI standard
private NotificationService notification;
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Spring processes @Autowired, @Resource, and @Inject through different BeanPostProcessors.** `AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor` handles `@Autowired` and `@Inject`. `CommonAnnotationBeanPostProcessor` handles `@Resource`.
- **@Resource name resolution includes JNDI lookup** in Java EE containers, making it useful for JNDI-bound DataSources in legacy application servers.
- **In Spring Boot 3, jakarta.inject replaces javax.inject.** If upgrading from Spring Boot 2.x, update the import from `javax.inject.Inject` to `jakarta.inject.Inject`.
- **GraalVM native images** work best with constructor injection because it avoids reflection. Field injection with `@Autowired` requires reflection hints in `reflect-config.json`.
- **The modern trend is no annotation at all.** The Spring team recommends single-constructor, no-annotation injection with Lombok `@RequiredArgsConstructor`. This is the dominant style in production Spring Boot applications.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What is the resolution order of `@Autowired`? (Type, @Qualifier, field name, @Primary, exception.)
- Can `@Resource` do constructor injection? (No, its @Target lacks PARAMETER.)
- What is the difference between `@Qualifier` and `@Named`? (@Qualifier is Spring, @Named is CDI; both serve the same purpose.)
- When do you actually need `@Autowired`? (Multiple constructors, `required=false` for optional deps.)
- Which BeanPostProcessor handles `@Resource`? (CommonAnnotationBeanPostProcessor.)
